  "notes": "テンパる comes from the mahjong term テンパイ ({聴牌|テンパイ}), meaning \"waiting for the winning tile.\" The sense of being \"on edge\" or \"at the limit\" evolved into the meaning of panicking.\n\nREGISTER:\nThis is casual slang widely used among younger speakers. Use {焦|あせ}る or パニックになる in more formal contexts.\n\nCOMMON PATTERNS:\n- テンパる: to panic/freak out\n- テンパっている: to be panicking (ongoing state)\n- テンパらないで: don't panic\n\nASPECT (ている):\nテンパっている indicates an ongoing state of being flustered or overwhelmed.",
